## Original Content
Schools and increasingly teen employers are drawing a hard line against phone use. The benefits for teens can be profound.

## Summary
Schools and a growing number of employers of teenagers are implementing strict phone-free policies in workplaces. Employers report that banning phones allows young workers to hyper-focus on tasks, and the benefits for teens' concentration and development may be significant.

## Key Points
- Teen employers are increasingly adopting hard-line policies against phone use on the job, following the lead of schools
- Employers report that phone-free environments allow young workers to 'hyper focus on the task at hand'
- Phone restrictions are described as a 'new frontier' extending beyond schools into the American workplace
- The benefits of phone-free work environments for teens are characterized as potentially profound


